# Line-ending policy.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows (`core.autocrlf=true`) and built on
# Linux and macOS, so without explicit rules git silently rewrites line
# endings on the way in and out of the object store. Two concrete
# consequences, both hit for real:
#
#  - THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt is generated verbatim from the bundled
#    crates' own licence files, and some of those ship CRLF. Normalising
#    them to LF on commit meant the committed copy could never match a
#    regeneration on Linux or macOS, which blocked four consecutive
#    release attempts with an unreadable "stale" error. `-text` keeps the
#    generator's exact bytes.
#
#  - A shell script committed with CRLF fails on Linux with
#    `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M`. install.sh is downloaded and piped
#    straight into `sh` by every macOS and Linux customer, so pinning it
#    to LF prevents a broken install that would never reproduce on the
#    machine it was authored on.

# Never touch the generated notices — byte-for-byte as cargo-about emits.
THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt -text

# Anything executed by a POSIX shell must be LF, always.
*.sh text eol=lf

# PowerShell is happiest with CRLF on the platform that runs it.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
